{"course": {"schema_version": "1.0", "course_id": "mitocw-es-260-spring-2006", "institution": "Massachusetts Institute of Technology", "platform": "MIT OpenCourseWare", "course_codes": ["ES.260"], "cross_listed_codes": [], "course_title": "Women's Novels: A Weekly Book Club", "course_slug": "es-260-womens-novels-a-weekly-book-club-spring-2006", "term": "Spring", "year": 2006, "as_taught_in": "Spring 2006", "language": "en", "departments": ["Experimental Study Group"], "academic_levels": ["Undergraduate"], "instructors": ["Faye Kasemset", "Dr. Holly Sweet", "Jocelyn Rodal"], "subject_primary": "Literature", "subject_secondary": ["Fiction", "Society", "Gender Studies", "Women's Studies"], "topic_tree": [{"name": "Humanities", "subtopics": [{"name": "Literature", "specialties": ["Fiction"]}]}, {"name": "Society", "subtopics": [{"name": "Gender Studies", "specialties": []}, {"name": "Women's Studies", "specialties": []}]}], "topic_keywords": [], "pedagogy_tags": [], "assessment_types": [], "course_feature_tags": [], "description": "This pass/fail seminar should be a fun setting where we can all enjoy a love of good books together. Students will read approximately one novel every two weeks, and the class will discuss each novel in a relaxed and interactive setting, with attention to whatever themes and issues interest them most about each book. We will read a wide mixture of classic and contemporary novels written by women, including: Edith Wharton, The House of Mirth ; Toni Morrison, Jazz ; Virginia Woolf, Mrs. Dalloway ; Alice Walker, The Color Purple ; Charlotte Brontë, Jane Eyre ; Sheri Reynolds, The Rapture of Canaan ; Jane Austen, Pride and Prejudice ; and Sylvia Plath, The Bell Jar . Recurrent issues likely to be discussed include: gender, race, and class; romance, love, and marriage; depression and suicide; and conception, childbirth, and parenthood.", "course_url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/es-260-womens-novels-a-weekly-book-club-spring-2006/", "image_url": "